Do not wait on a wal pusher that has already gone.

A node with a dirty buffer could hang forever on shutdown. The postmaster
stops every background worker before the checkpointer runs the shutdown
checkpoint, the wal pusher is a background worker, and the checkpointer's
write of a dirty buffer called ZouWalWaitForLSN. So the last process with
work to do waited for a durable LSN that the only process able to publish
one had already left the building, and the postmaster sat there with
nothing alive to finish it (zou #468).

Two halves, because either alone leaves a way to hang.

The wait was owed to the page object the write was about to store, so
that a page never reaches the store ahead of the WAL that produced it.
With the page service on, which is the default, a checkpointer write
stores no page at all: the crate elides the eager put because pages past
the checkpoint chain are reconstructed from the stream instead. A barrier
for an object nobody writes is a wait for nothing, so zouwritev now asks
the crate the same question the crate asks itself and skips it. The
answer comes from zou_pageserve_on rather than from getenv here so that
the shim and the shim's caller cannot disagree about it.

The other half is that no wait should be infinite when the thing that
ends it cannot happen. The pusher publishes its pid in shared memory when
it starts mirroring and clears it on the way out, and a waiter that finds
a pusher that has been and gone gives up with an error after ten seconds
instead of sleeping forever. The grace period is there because the
postmaster restarts a pusher that died immediately, and a write should
sit through that rather than fail over it. The sleep becomes a timed one
so the check gets a chance to run.

The error ends differently for the two callers, and both endings are the
right one. A page write fails, which is the point of the barrier: a page
whose WAL never reached the store must not reach it either. A committing
backend is inside the delayChkpt critical section, so the error is
promoted to PANIC and the cluster restarts, which is what Postgres does
when a WAL write fails, and the restart brings up a fresh pusher rather
than leaving a node that can neither commit nor stop. It happens before
TransactionIdCommitTree, so no committed xid is ever aborted.

